WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 281: Louis Armstrong: Satchmo, the Hot Five, and the King of New Orleans Jazz

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:19.600
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series celebrates the musical genius whose golden horn, gravel voice, and infectious smile created the soundtrack of modern American culture.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.000
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of Louis Armstrong.

5
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:43.520
[George] Born on August 4, 1901, in the destitute, violence-ridden neighborhood of New Orleans known as the Battlefield, Louis Armstrong was the son of Mary Albert, a 16-year-old domestic maid, and William Armstrong, who abandoned the family shortly after Louis' birth.

6
00:00:43.520 --> 00:01:00.640
[George] Raised in deep poverty, young Louis scavenged coal yards, delivered coal to brothels in Storyville, and worked for the Karnowsky family, Lithuanian Jewish immigrants who gave him warm meals and advanced him $5 to purchase his first battered brass cornet from a pawn shop.

7
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:11.200
[George] On New Year's Eve 1912, 11-year-old Louis fired his stepfather's six-shooter revolver into the night sky to celebrate and was immediately arrested by police.

8
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:21.520
[George] Sentenced to the colored waif's home for boys, Louis came under the discipline of bandmaster Peter Davis, who handed him a clean bugle and taught him basic music theory.

9
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:30.800
[George] Within a year, Louis was leader of the Waif's home brass band, playing so brilliantly that crowds followed the boys through the streets of New Orleans.

10
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:44.960
[George] The Battlefield section of New Orleans was a notoriously rough, impoverished four block district bordered by rampart and perdito streets, where street fights, police raids, and razor blade brawls were everyday occurrences.

11
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] The Karnofsky family, immigrant peddlers who collected junk and delivered coal, treated young Louis like a son, seating him at their family dinner table for Sabbath meals and teaching him Yiddish songs that instilled in him an eternal empathy for the oppressed Jewish people.

12
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:16.920
[George] At the Colored Waf's home, bandmaster Peter Davis was initially skeptical of the mischievous boy, but soon recognized that Louis possessed an extraordinary natural ear, capable of memorizing complex brass marches and blues melodies after hearing them only once.

13
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:30.280
[George] Released from the home, Armstrong honed his craft playing in rough honkytonks, marching funeral parades, and Mississippi Riverboats in Fate Maribel's orchestra, where he learned to read complex orchestral arrangements.

14
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:37.640
[George] His undisputed musical idol and surrogate father was Joe King Oliver, the premier cornetist of New Orleans.

15
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:47.880
[George] In August 1922, King Oliver sent a telegram summoning 21-year-old Louis to Chicago to join his Creole Jazz band at the Lincoln Gardens on the south side.

16
00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.800
[George] Chicago was electrified.

17
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:59.960
[George] Oliver on lead cornet and Armstrong on Second Cornet played lightning fast improvised two-horn breaks that left white and black musicians alike breathless with astonishment.

18
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:19.160
[George] In 1924, urged by his ambitious pianist wife Lil Hardin, Armstrong moved to New York City to join Fletcher Henderson's prestigious ballroom orchestra, where Louis's swinging syncopation and rich brass tone transformed Henderson's arrangers and permanently altered the sound of New York big band music.

19
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:33.960
[George] Playing on the paddlewheel steamships of the Streckfest line aboard the SS Capitol under Fate Marable, Armstrong mastered the art of sight-reading written sheet music, studying arrangements under the boat's steam chandeliers as they sailed from New Orleans to St.

20
00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:34.680
[George] Paul.

21
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:52.720
[George] King Oliver's Creole Jazz Band at the Lincoln Gardens on the south side of Chicago was a musical phenomenon that drew Chicago mobsters, young white college students like Bix Baderbeck, and prominent classical conductors who sat transfixed by Oliver and Armstrong's intuitive telepathy.

22
00:03:52.720 --> 00:04:07.920
[George] When Armstrong joined Fletcher Henderson's orchestra at the Roseland Ballroom in New York in 1924, his loose, relaxed blues inflection shattered the rigid, mechanical rhythms of Eastern dance bands, teaching New York musicians how to swing.

23
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:20.480
[George] Returning to Chicago in 1925, Armstrong entered the OK Recording Studio to produce a series of 75 historic sides with his small studio groups, the Hot Five and Hot Seven.

24
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.880
[George] These recordings are the foundational bedrock of modern American music.

25
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.440
[George] Prior to Armstrong, New Orleans jazz was collective polyphony.

26
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:39.920
[George] Armstrong transformed it into an individual soloist's art, proving that an improvised solo could possess the structural genius and emotional sublimity of classical chamber music.

27
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:46.960
[George] In February 1926, while recording Heebie Jeebes, the sheet music fell off his music stand.

28
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:59.120
[George] Rather than stopping the take, Armstrong began vocalizing spontaneous nonsense syllables, eef, gaffe, bop, scatten, inventing recorded scat singing and scoring a runaway national hit.

29
00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:11.920
[George] His 1928 masterpiece, West End Blues, opening with a dazzling, unaccompanied cornet cadenza that scaled high C with architectural perfection, remains the supreme masterpiece of recorded jazz.

30
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.600
[George] The Hot Five recording sessions, produced by Richard M.

31
00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:26.400
[George] Jones for OK Records in Chicago, were captured on primitive wax cylinders and acoustic horns, yet they unleashed a musical revolution that swept across the globe.

32
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:45.080
[George] Playing alongside his brilliant wife Lil Harden on piano, Johnny Dodds on clarinet, Kid Ory on trombone, and Johnny Sincseer on banjo, Armstrong demonstrated that an improvised jazz solo could possess the dramatic arc, harmonic logic, and emotional depth of a Beethoven sonata.

33
00:05:45.080 --> 00:06:00.600
[George] In West End blues, his soaring opening cadenza, cascading through complex chromatic intervals before settling into a mournful, blues-drenched trumpet melody, established a gold standard of virtuosity that altered the course of Western instrumental music.

34
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:09.480
[George] Transitioning from the cornet to the brighter, more piercing trumpet, Armstrong became a worldwide commercial superstar in the 1930s.

35
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:23.640
[George] He starred in Broadway reviews such as Fats Waller's, Hot Chocolates, introducing his gravelly, intimate vocal interpretations of the great American songbook, including Ain't Misbehavin, and I Can't Give You Anything But Love.

36
00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:34.840
[George] In live stage performances, Armstrong routinely blew audiences away by hitting 100 consecutive high C's before capping his solo with a soaring, earth-shaking high F.

37
00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:51.240
[George] His revolutionary approach to phrasing, floating behind the beat, stretching syllables, and injecting subtle microtonal blue notes, became the universal grammar copied by every American singer who followed, from Billy Holiday and Frank Sinatra to Ella Fitzgerald.

38
00:06:51.240 --> 00:07:03.640
[George] As the Great Depression deepened, Armstrong's heroic trumpet playing became a beacon of joy and resilience for millions of Americans who listened to his late night broadcasts from the Cotton Club and the Savoy Ballroom.

39
00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:09.560
[George] His jaw-dropping vocal interpretations dismantled traditional notions of Bel Canto singing.

40
00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:22.000
[George] Using his raspy, gravelly baritone, Armstrong bent pitches, swallowed vowels, and fractured melodic lines, inventing modern popular vocal phrasing and directly inspiring Billy Holliday.

41
00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.040
[George] Bing Crosby and Frank Sinatra.

42
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:39.120
[George] His ability to sustain dozens of blistering high seas in live theater performances pushed the physical limits of brass playing, requiring him to use special medicated lip salves and diamond-tipped mouthpieces to preserve his shredded embouchure.

43
00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:52.000
[George] During the Cold War, the United States State Department sent Armstrong and his All Stars band on extensive international goodwill tour across Europe, Africa, and Asia, christening him Ambassador Satch.

44
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:59.120
[George] In Ghana in 1956, over 100,000 ecstatic fans packed the Across Stadium to welcome him.

45
00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:10.000
[George] While some younger black activists criticized his broad comedic smile as deferential to white audiences, Armstrong proved his fierce moral courage in September 1957.

46
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:24.320
[George] When Arkansas Governor Orville Faubus deployed the National Guard to block nine black students from integrating Little Rock Central High School, Armstrong erupted in fury, telling reporters, the way they are treating my people in the South.

47
00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.320
[George] The government can go to hell.

48
00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:33.760
[George] He publicly called President Eisenhower Too Faced, and announced he would cancel his State Department tour of the Soviet Union.

49
00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:40.480
[George] His courageous public protest forced Eisenhower to send the 101st Airborne to protect the Little Rock Nine.

50
00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:52.320
[George] Armstrong's international tour during the Cold War were monumental cultural triumphs, as European, African, and Asian audiences recognized his authentic genius and warm human spirit.

51
00:08:52.320 --> 00:09:05.080
[George] In the Belgian Congo in 1960, opposing tribal factions in a bloody civil war declared a temporary one-day ceasefire so both sides could attend Armstrong's concert in Leopoldville without violence.

52
00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:25.880
[George] His courageous public denunciation of President Eisenhower over the Little Rock school desegregation crisis demonstrated that underneath his smiling entertainer persona beat the heart of a proud, fearless black man who refused to allow his government to mistreat black children while holding him up as an international symbol of American freedom.

53
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:37.400
[George] In May 1964, at the height of Beatlemania when the British rock group occupied the top three spots on the Billboard charts, 62-year-old Louis Armstrong shocked the music industry.

54
00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:41.240
[George] His recording of Hello Dolly surged to number one.

55
00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:47.960
[George] Knocking the Beatles off the top of the pop charts and making Armstrong the oldest artist ever to score a Billboard No.

56
00:09:49.080 --> 00:10:04.840
[George] In 1967, amid the urban riots and escalating bloodshed of the Vietnam War, Armstrong recorded What a Wonderful World, a tender, luminous ballad celebrating the simple beauty of green trees, red roses and babies crying.

57
00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:18.760
[George] While ABC Records executives disliked the track and refused to promote it in America, the song became a global smash hit, topping the charts in Great Britain and becoming Armstrong's signature international anthem of hope.

58
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:34.440
[George] The recording of Hello, Dolly, was initially intended as a simple commercial demo to promote the upcoming Broadway musical starring Carol Channing, but Armstrong's infectious enthusiasm and swinging gravel voice turned it into a runaway global phenomenon.

59
00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:50.560
[George] In December 1967, recording What a Wonderful World in New York City, Armstrong insisted on continuing the session despite furious interruptions from ABC Records president Larry Newton, who despised the ballad and tried to shut down the recording studio.

60
00:10:50.800 --> 00:11:04.160
[George] Armstrong envisioned the song as a healing balm for a fractured world torn apart by political assassinations and war, singing each word with a profound, tender gratitude that resonated in the hearts of millions across the globe.

61
00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:19.440
[George] Despite his colossal international fame and wealth, Armstrong lived with profound humility, choosing to spend his final thirty years living in a modest brick home in a working-class black neighborhood in Corona, Queens, with his devoted wife Lucille.

62
00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:30.400
[George] He sat on his front porch eating red beans and rice, played trumpet for neighborhood children on the sidewalk, and answered thousands of fan letters by hand on his portable typewriter.

63
00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:39.600
[George] Passing away peacefully in his sleep in July 1971 at age 69, the entire world mourned the loss of music's greatest ambassador.

64
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.600
[George] Duke Ellington stated the absolute truth of Armstrong's legacy.

65
00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:47.040
[George] If anybody was a master, it was Louis Armstrong.

66
00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.600
[George] He was born poor, died rich, and never hurt anyone on the way.

67
00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:59.440
[George] Louis Armstrong's Golden Horn gave birth to modern American music, a sound that will echo across human history for all time.

68
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:19.200
[George] When international journalists asked Armstrong why a wealthy global superstar chose to live in a modest brick row house in Corona, Queens, Louis would smile and explain that he loved his neighbors, loved sitting on the front stoop with his horn, and had no desire to isolate himself behind iron gates in Beverly Hills.

69
00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:31.880
[George] His living room was a vibrant salon where neighborhood kids, mail carriers, and visiting European classical conductors gathered around his tape recorders to listen to music and eat Lucille's home-cooked meals.

70
00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:35.800
[George] Louis Armstrong's legacy is the very soul of American music.

71
00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:46.760
[George] He took the sorrow of slavery, the joy of the New Orleans streets, and the boundless freedom of the human spirit, turning them into a golden horn of universal brotherhood.

72
00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:07.960
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the aristocratic jazz Maestro Duke Ellington, the master of American orchestration and the Cotton Club.

73
00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:10.920
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

